From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] clk: sunxi-ng: a733: the video engine's PLLs, module clocks, gates and resets

[Recipe cut: pll-ve0 and pll-ve1 are NOT CLK_IS_CRITICAL here: the flag only
serves the vendor video-engine driver, which this recipe does not carry, so
without it the VCOs stay in the state the boot chain leaves them in (see the
comment in the diff).]

The video engine is the last big block with nothing in our CCU. PLL_VE0,
PLL_VE1, VE_ENC0_CLK, VE_DEC_CLK, VE_BGR and the engines' six bus-matrix bits
keep whatever the boot chain left, and no consumer can claim them. This patch
adds fourteen clocks and two resets for the two engines -- a decoder and an
encoder, each with its own PLL -- and repoints NPU_CLK's two VE mux slots at
the new PLL taps. Nothing in the tree references the new ids until the device
tree in `arm64-dts-a733-ve-decoder-and-encoder-nodes` does.

It is step 1 of 3 of the vendor video-engine path: the SoC glue. The driver is
the vendor's cedar_ve (`soc-sunxi-cedar-ve-carry-the-vendor-video-engine-interface`),
the userspace part is Allwinner's closed-source one.

WHERE THE VALUES COME FROM
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The CCU field tables of the A733 user manual (UM v0.92 4.1.6), every field
re-read in the UM text, and every register again in UM V1.00 section 4.1 --
same fields, same defaults. CCU base 0x02002000.

  PLL_VE0_CTRL_REG    0x0220  reset 0x4030_5A00
  PLL_VE1_CTRL_REG    0x0240  reset 0x4020_5600
    31 PLL_EN  30 PLL_LDO_EN (reset 1)  29 LOCK_ENABLE (reset 0)  28 LOCK (R)
    27 PLL_OUTPUT_GATE  22:20 PLL_OUTPUT_DIV, P0 = field + 1
    15:8 PLL_N, N = field + 1, lower bound 11 on the field  1 PLL_INPUT_DIV, M1
    VE0PLL = InputFreq*N/M1/P0; with a 26 MHz DCXO the register descriptions give
    546 MHz (VE0PLL) and 696 MHz (VE1PLL) as defaults
  Table 4-2, both rows: Fvco 1.26G~2.52G, 1/2, typical 1.872G, P0 1~8, lock
    0.1 ms, reference 24MHz
  Table 4-3: VE0 24 / N 91 / 2184 / 546, VE1 24 / N 87 / 2088 / 696
  Figure 4-2: PLL_VE0 and PLL_VE1 feed "VE_DEC, VE_ENC0, NPU"
  VE_ENC0_CLK_REG     0x0A80  31 gate, 26:24 mux (000 VE0PLL, 001 VE1PLL, ...),
                              4:0 M
  VE_DEC_CLK_REG      0x0A88  the same, but 000 VE1PLL, 001 VE0PLL
  VE_BGR_REG          0x0A8C  18 VE_DEC_RST, 16 VE_ENC0_RST, 2 VE_DEC_GATING,
                              0 VE_ENC0_GATING
  AHB_MAT_CLK_GATING  0x05C0  1 VE_ENC_AHB, 0 VE_DEC_AHB, reset 1
  MBUS_MAT_CLK_GATING 0x05E0  14 VE_DEC_MBUS, 12 VE_ENC0_MBUS, reset 1
  MBUS_GATE_EN        0x05E4  18 VE_DEC_MCLK_EN, 1 VE_ENC0_MCLK_EN, reset 0

The parent is pll-ref, 24.000000 MHz (the 26 MHz DCXO x 96 / 104, `arm64-dts-a733-model-the-26mhz-dcxo` and
`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-feed-pll-ref-from-the-dcxo`). Table 4-2 gives 24MHz as the reference for both PLLs, and the
register's own note is arithmetic that only 24 satisfies: 24 x 91 / 4 = 546,
where 26 would give 591.5. So for the factors the board is found in, this
model computes 546 MHz (VE0PLL) and 696 MHz (VE1PLL) from the UM and pll-ref;
the vendor's clk debugfs prints the same 546, and that agreement is not the
source.

SILICON WITNESS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Measured: the 8 GiB board, vendor kernel
6.6.98-4-aw2511, an engine requested through cedar_ve (IOCTL_ENGINE_REQ),
read via /dev/mem, released, nothing written:

  register        idle        decoder req.  encoder req.
  PLL_VE0_CTRL    0x01305a00  0xf9305a00    as idle
  PLL_VE1_CTRL    0x01205600  as idle       0xf9205600
  AHB_MAT         0x11010390  0x11010391    0x11010392
  MBUS_MAT        0xf0010003  0xf0014003    0xf0011003
  MBUS_GATE_EN    0x00000805  0x00040805    0x00000807
  VE_ENC0_CLK     0x01000000  as idle       0x81000000
  VE_DEC_CLK      0x01000000  0x81000000    as idle
  VE_BGR          0x00000000  0x00040004    0x00010001
  VEPLL_GATE_EN   0x00030000  0x00030000    0x00030000

Every bit this patch models moves exactly with its engine and nothing else in
those registers does: the bit map above is witnessed, and each PLL has one
consumer. Our own kernel, without this patch, on the same board: PLL_VE0
0x41305a00, PLL_VE1 0x41205600, AHB_MAT 0x110103bf, MBUS_MAT 0xf1015803,
MBUS_GATE_EN 0x00000804, both module clocks 0, VEPLL_GATE_EN 0x00030003.

DECISIONS, each argued at its site in the code
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  - PLL_VE0/PLL_VE1 are a ccu_nm VCO plus a P0 tap each (as PLL_NPU and
    PLL_GPU0), parent pll-ref, VCO window 1272-2520 MHz (`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-npu-pll-module-clock-and-resets`'s floor).
  - The two VCOs are CLK_IS_CRITICAL. Mainline's ccu_nm_enable() does not do
    UM Method 2 (lock before output gate); the vendor driver toggles every VE
    clock per engine request and per rate change, and `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-pll-gpu0-must-be-running-before-the-gpu-binds` measured what a
    PLL transition under a clocked block does on this SoC. The VCOs run
    locked from registration on; the taps follow the consumer. Cost: two
    VCOs running while the engines sleep.
  - The module clocks carry CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT | CLK_SET_RATE_NO_REPARENT |
    CLK_SET_RATE_GATE: a rate moves the whole chain, never the mux, and only
    while the engine's clock is unprepared.
  - The module clocks are capped at 624 MHz (.common.max_rate, applied by
    ccu_common with clk_hw_set_rate_range()). VDD-VE is DCDC2 at 800 mV,
    shared with the DRAM; the vendor's VF table pairs every bin with 624 MHz
    at 800 mV; Table 4-2's typical VCO 1.872G is 3 x 624. The vendor enforces
    none of it -- see below.
  - The eight bus gates carry flags 0: the vendor driver names all four per
    engine in the device tree and enables them before use (`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-gpu-bus-matrix-gates-are-critical`'s GPU gates
    had no consumer; these do).
  - LDO_EN and LOCK_ENABLE are forced in probe() for both PLLs, before
    registration enables them.
  - NPU_CLK's slots 100/101 become .hw pointers to ve0pll/ve1pll. A name
    lookup would have found "pll-ve0", the VCO, and handed the NPU mux the
    wrong rate. The NPU comment that explained the placeholders is rewritten;
    its encoding-111 warning stays.

WHERE THE VENDOR DRIVER DIFFERS, AND WHAT THIS PATCH DOES (its ccu-sun60iw2.c, read from the
BSP copy on a board on the vendor kernel, which matches a second copy line for line in the
VE parts; a hint, never a source)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  - PLL_INPUT_DIV (M1) not modelled; P0 folded into the ccu_nm's ".m". A
    boot that left M1 = 2 would be reported at twice its rate.
  - no ceiling: cedar_ve accepts IOCTL_SET_VE_FREQ up to 900 MHz
    (VE_CLK_HIGH_WATER) and passes it to clk_set_rate(), and an engine whose
    rate nobody set runs at its PLL's reset factors -- measured above: the
    requested encoder at 696 MHz, over the vendor's own 624 MHz table.
  - its six matrix gates are CLK_IS_CRITICAL "key" gates that OR
    0x10000FF / 0x41055800 / 0x40302 into every write of 0x05C0 / 0x05E0 /
    0x05E4. The UM has no key field there; the constants are other gates,
    re-opened by whoever writes. And the vendor kernel reads them clear at
    idle, which that source cannot produce: the running vendor CCU is not the
    BSP copy, so its flags are no evidence.
  - CLK_IGNORE_UNUSED on both PLLs; a sigma-delta pattern model that 624 MHz
    does not need (integer N; PAT0 reads 0).
  What the vendor gets RIGHT and mainline lacks: its PLL enable helper does
  Method 2. CLK_IS_CRITICAL on the VCO is the substitute here.

A523: one pll-ve (0x058, a single ccu_nkmp) and one engine. Nothing carried
over; no A523 offset, bit or bound is used.

NOT MODELLED, on purpose
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  - VEPLL_GATE_EN_REG 0x1918: software gates at reset 1 on
    both stacks, auto-gating off on ours (0x00030003) and on in the vendor's
    (0x00030000) in every state. Both pass; no write needed. `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-npu-pll-module-clock-and-resets`'s call for
    NPUPLL_GATE_EN.
  - PLL_VEn_LOCK_CTRL/PAT0/PAT1/BIAS (0x0224-0x0230, 0x0244-0x0250): lock
    window and fractional pattern, unused at an integer N.

WHAT THIS CHANGES ON A KERNEL WITH NO VE DRIVER BOUND
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  - PLL_VE0/PLL_VE1: LDO_EN and LOCK_ENABLE set in probe, PLL_EN set at
    registration (critical): both VCOs run at their reset factors, 2184 and
    2088 MHz, locked, with their output gates closed.
  - clk_disable_unused() clears AHB_MAT bits 0/1 and MBUS_MAT bits 12/14,
    1 -> 0 -- VE-only fields, and the state the vendor leaves them in. The
    other VE bits are 0 already.

BINDING IDS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Allocated and counted in the applied tree:
clocks 97-110 (CLK_PLL_VE0, CLK_VE0PLL, CLK_PLL_VE1, CLK_VE1PLL, CLK_VE_ENC0,
CLK_VE_DEC, CLK_BUS_VE_ENC0, CLK_BUS_VE_DEC, CLK_AHB_VE_ENC0, CLK_AHB_VE_DEC,
CLK_MBUS_VE_ENC0, CLK_MBUS_VE_DEC, CLK_MCLK_VE_ENC0, CLK_MCLK_VE_DEC), resets
37-38 (RST_BUS_VE_ENC0, RST_BUS_VE_DEC). A binding-id consistency check passed
over all eight headers.

VERIFIED / NOT VERIFIED
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  applies      full series, freshly unpacked 6.18.36, patch --fuzz=0.
  builds       ccu-sun60iw2.o, native, W=1: no warning.
  arrays       read out of the built object, sizes predicted first:
                 sun60iw2_ccu_clks    784 B = 98 entries (was 84, +14)
                 sun60iw2_hw_clks     896 B = 8 + 111 * 8, so
                                      .num == CLK_MCLK_VE_DEC + 1
                 sun60iw2_ccu_resets  312 B = 39 entries (was 37, +2)
               Both resets are IN the map, not only in the header -- the
               RST_BUS_GMAC0 silent-no-op trap.
  HW-effect    NOT OBSERVED. The register reads above are input evidence,
               not proof of this patch.

PENDING OBSERVABLE (falsifier), on a kernel with this patch and `arm64-dts-a733-ve-decoder-and-encoder-nodes`:
  - before any VE use: abs 0x02002220 and 0x02002240 read bits 31/30/29/28
    set and bit 27 clear; abs 0x020025C0 bits 0/1 and 0x020025E0 bits 12/14
    read 0.
  - after the driver has probed: abs 0x02002220 N = 78 (0x4D), P0 = 3 (field
    2), 0x02002A88 = 0x01000000 -- 624 MHz on VE0PLL, gate closed; likewise
    0x02002240 / 0x02002A80 for the encoder.
  - with an engine requested: exactly the bits of the witness table above
    set for that engine, and /sys/kernel/debug/clk/ve-dec/clk_rate 624000000.
  - a request of 900 MHz through IOCTL_SET_VE_FREQ ends at 624000000.

Assisted-by: Claude
---

diff --git a/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60iw2.c b/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60iw2.c
--- a/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60iw2.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60iw2.c
@@ -1147,6 +1147,203 @@ static SUNXI_CCU_M_DATA_WITH_MUX_GATE(de0_clk, "de0", de0_parents, 0x0A00,
  */
 static SUNXI_CCU_GATE_HWS(bus_de0_clk, "bus-de0", ahb_hws, 0x0A04, BIT(0), 0);
 
+/*
+ * The video engine's two PLLs. Four clocks, each naming one register field:
+ *
+ *   pll-ve0  PLL_VE0_CTRL 0x0220 / abs 0x02002220 -- the VCO, N [15:8] and
+ *            M1 [1], enable bit 31, lock bit 28
+ *   ve0pll   the same register's single output tap, P0 [22:20] gated by
+ *            PLL_OUTPUT_GATE bit 27
+ *   pll-ve1  PLL_VE1_CTRL 0x0240 / abs 0x02002240, the same fields
+ *
+ *   ve1pll   its tap
+ *
+ * They sit here, before the NPU, and not with the engines further down,
+ * because NPU_CLK's mux names both taps and needs them declared first.
+ *
+ * VE0PLL = InputFreq*N/M1/P0 with N = PLL_N + 1, and the lower bound on PLL_N
+ * (11) applies to the FIELD, so
+ * N >= 12; M1 = PLL_INPUT_DIV + 1 over 0..1; P0 =
+ * PLL_OUTPUT_DIV + 1 over 0..7. PLL_VE1 reads the same in
+ * the A733 user manual. Split into VCO and tap for the reason PLL_NPU and
+ * PLL_GPU0 are: ccu_nm models N and M1 only, P0 is a divider of its own.
+ *
+ * THE INPUT IS 24 MHz, AND THE UM PROVES IT ARITHMETICALLY. Table 4-2 gives
+ * both rows a 24MHz reference, and each register carries
+ * a worked check: a 26 MHz DCXO is stated to give a default output of 546 MHz
+ * (A733 user manual). The reset word 0x4030_5A00 is N = 91, P0 = 4, and
+ * 24 x 91 / 4 = 546 where 26 x 91 / 4 would be 591.5 -- so the parent is
+ * PLL_REF, not the DCXO. PLL_VE1's 0x4020_5600 is N = 87, P0 = 3, and
+ * 24 x 87 / 3 = 696, its note's number. Both are also Table
+ * 4-3's rows: VE0_PLL 24 / N 91 / VCO 2184 / OUT 546 and VE1_PLL 24 / N 87 /
+ * VCO 2088 / OUT 696.
+ *
+ * In this tree that parent is pll-ref, and pll-ref is 24.000000 MHz: the
+ * 26 MHz DCXO x 96 / 104 (`arm64-dts-a733-model-the-26mhz-dcxo`, `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-feed-pll-ref-from-the-dcxo`; Table 4-3's REF_PLL row). The DCXO
+ * feeds PLL_REF and nothing else here, so these PLLs count in 24, not 26 --
+ * the 24/26 mixup, checked rather than assumed. For the
+ * factors the board is found in (below) this model therefore computes 546 MHz
+ * for VE0PLL and 696 MHz for VE1PLL, from the UM and pll-ref. The vendor's
+ * clk debugfs prints the same 546; that agreement is not the source.
+ *
+ * VCO WINDOW. Table 4-2 reads 1.26G~2.52G, pre-divider 1/2, P0 1~8, lock time
+ * 0.1 ms for both -- PLL_NPU's window, so `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-npu-pll-module-clock-and-resets`'s floor
+ * applies for `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-npu-pll-module-clock-and-resets`'s reason: 1272 = 24 x 53, because ccu_nm_set_rate() does
+ * not clamp and a 1260 MHz floor would be programmed as 24 x 52 = 1248.
+ * The table's TYPICAL VCO for both rows is 1.872G, which is not the reset
+ * rate -- it is 3 x 624 MHz, the operating point the module clocks below are
+ * capped at and the device tree asks for (`arm64-dts-a733-ve-decoder-and-encoder-nodes`).
+ *
+ * NEITHER VCO IS CLK_IS_CRITICAL IN THIS TREE, because nothing in it
+ * enables a video-engine clock: there is no VE driver, so a critical VCO would
+ * only run for nothing, and left alone both stay in the state the boot chain
+ * hands over (PLL_EN clear). A VE driver must bring the flag back with it (or
+ * an enable helper that follows Method 2), for the reason that follows; it is
+ * a decision about the enable SEQUENCE, not about who uses them. UM 4.1.4.1 Method 2
+ * opens PLL_OUTPUT_GATE only after LOCK and a further 20 us. Mainline's
+ * ccu_nm_enable() sets PLL_EN and returns without waiting, and the tap's gate
+ * is opened straight after it; with PLL_EN as the clock's enable, every
+ * consumer enable would hand the engine an unlocked output for up to the
+ * 0.1 ms lock time. On this SoC that class of failure is measured: a PLL_GPU0
+ * transition under a clocked GPU stops the SoC without a word, which is why
+ * `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-pll-gpu0-must-be-running-before-the-gpu-binds` made PLL_GPU0 critical. And the VE driver makes the transition
+ * routine -- the vendor's cedar_ve.c disables every VE clock on the last
+ * IOCTL_ENGINE_REL and around every rate change (disable_cedar_hw_clk(),
+ * setup_ve_freq()) and enables them again on the next IOCTL_ENGINE_REQ.
+ * The vendor survives that because its own PLL enable helper does Method 2;
+ * the silicon shows it switching PLL_EN, and even PLL_LDO_EN, per request
+ * (table below). Mainline has no such helper, so the transition is kept off
+ * the VCO instead: critical keeps each VCO running and locked from
+ * registration on, and only the output gate -- the tap -- follows the
+ * consumer, which is Method 2's order.
+ * Mainline's A523 driver reaches the same state by forcing PLL_EN on every
+ * PLL in probe and presenting the output gate as the enable
+ * (ccu-sun55i-a523.c, sun55i_a523_ccu_probe()). The cost is two VCOs that
+ * run while the engines sleep.
+ *
+ * A RATE STILL MOVES ONLY WITH THE OUTPUT GATED: the module clocks carry
+ * CLK_SET_RATE_GATE, so while an engine holds its clock prepared a rate
+ * request is refused instead of relocking the PLL under it.
+ *
+ * SWEPT FOR OTHER CONSUMERS. Exactly three CCU registers name VE0PLL or
+ * VE1PLL in a mux: VE_ENC0_CLK, VE_DEC_CLK and NPU_CLK -- every register in
+ * the A733 user manual whose field enum carries either string, and
+ * UM figure 4-2's list, "VE_DEC, VE_ENC0, NPU" for both PLLs.
+ * NPU_CLK is pinned to NPUPLL by the NPU node's assigned-clock-parents,
+ * and `arm64-dts-a733-ve-decoder-and-encoder-nodes` pins VE_DEC to VE0PLL and VE_ENC0 to VE1PLL. Each PLL has one
+ * consumer, so a rate request on one engine cannot reach the other.
+ *
+ * WHAT THE BOARD IS FOUND IN, all read-only on the 8 GiB board:
+ *
+ *   state                                     PLL_VE0_CTRL  PLL_VE1_CTRL
+ *   ours, no VE model (test run, 09-17)    0x41305a00    0x41205600
+ *   vendor 6.6.98-4-aw2511, VE idle           0x01305a00    0x01205600
+ *   vendor, decoder requested (09-21)         0xf9305a00    as idle
+ *   vendor, encoder requested (09-21)         as idle       0xf9205600
+ *
+ * (read on the board; the
+ * vendor rows were read the same way -- the
+ * engine requested through the vendor driver with IOCTL_ENGINE_REQ, read,
+ * released, nothing written.) Ours is the reset word plus bit 24, the
+ * reserved bit `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-npu-pll-module-clock-and-resets` already decoded as a power-on value on PLL_NPU,
+ * PLL_VIDEO1 and PLL_DE; nothing here writes it, and probe() and
+ * ccu_nm_set_rate() both read-modify-write. The vendor leaves PLL_LDO_EN
+ * CLEAR at idle -- `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-npu-pll-module-clock-and-resets`'s finding on PLL_NPU, again -- so probe() forces it
+ * instead of trusting the reset default. A requested engine has bits 31..27
+ * set on its own PLL -- PLL_EN, LDO_EN, LOCK_ENABLE, LOCK, OUTPUT_GATE --
+ * with the factor fields untouched, and the other engine's PLL stays off:
+ * the one-consumer claim above, witnessed. Untouched factors mean the vendor
+ * runs an engine whose rate nobody set at 546 MHz (decoder) and 696 MHz
+ * (encoder); the module clocks below say what this tree does instead.
+ *
+ * NOT MODELLED: VEPLL_GATE_EN_REG 0x1918 / abs 0x02003918, a second gate
+ * stage behind each tap -- bits 17/16 VEnPLL_GATE_SW_CFG (reset 1 =
+ * Enable), bits 1/0 VEnPLL_AUTO_GATE_EN (reset 1 = No-Auto), register reset
+ * 0x0003_0003. Our boot reads the reset value
+ * (0x00030003, read on the board); the vendor reads 0x00030000 idle
+ * AND with either engine running, i.e. auto-gating on, the software gates at
+ * their reset value. The software gates pass in both states, so neither
+ * needs a write from us -- the call `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-npu-pll-module-clock-and-resets` made for NPUPLL_GATE_EN.
+ *
+ * WHAT THE VENDOR DOES DIFFERENTLY (its ccu-sun60iw2.c is a hint, never a
+ * source):
+ *   - one ccu_nm per PLL with P0 folded in as its ".m" (_SUNXI_CCU_DIV(20, 3))
+ *     and PLL_INPUT_DIV not modelled at all, so a boot that left M1 = 2 would
+ *     be reported at twice its rate. Here M1 is modelled and P0 is the tap.
+ *   - a sigma-delta pattern on PAT0/PAT1 (0x0228/0x022C, 0x0248/0x024C). Not
+ *     modelled: 624 MHz is an integer N, PAT0 reads 0 under both kernels in
+ *     the 09-17 dumps, and `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-gpu-pll-module-clock-bus-gates-and-reset` left PLL_GPU0's pattern registers out for
+ *     the same reason.
+ *   - CLK_IGNORE_UNUSED plus a Method-2 enable helper of its own
+ *     (ccu_pll_gate_helper_enable(): output gate off, LDO, PLL_EN,
+ *     LOCK_ENABLE, wait for LOCK, output gate on). Mainline has no such
+ *     helper; CLK_IS_CRITICAL on the VCO is the substitute.
+ *
+ * A523: its single pll-ve (0x058, one ccu_nkmp, CLK_SET_RATE_GATE) carries
+ * nothing over. The A733 has two PLLs at 0x0220/0x0240 and two engines, and
+ * no A523 offset, bit or bound is used here.
+ */
+#define SUN60IW2_PLL_VE0_REG		0x0220
+#define SUN60IW2_PLL_VE1_REG		0x0240
+
+/* Table 4-2's window with `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-npu-pll-module-clock-and-resets`'s floor: 1272 = 24 x 53, 2520 = 24 x 105. */
+#define SUN60IW2_PLL_VE_MIN_VCO		1272000000U
+#define SUN60IW2_PLL_VE_MAX_VCO		2520000000U
+
+/* PLL_VEn_CTRL bits that are not modelled as clock gates; forced in probe(). */
+#define SUN60IW2_PLL_VE_LDO_EN		BIT(30)
+#define SUN60IW2_PLL_VE_LOCK_ENABLE	BIT(29)
+
+static struct ccu_nm pll_ve0_clk = {
+	.enable		= BIT(31),
+	.lock		= BIT(28),
+	.n		= _SUNXI_CCU_MULT_OFFSET_MIN_MAX(8, 8, 1, 12, 255),
+	.m		= _SUNXI_CCU_DIV(1, 1),
+	.min_rate	= SUN60IW2_PLL_VE_MIN_VCO,
+	.max_rate	= SUN60IW2_PLL_VE_MAX_VCO,
+	.common		= {
+		.reg		= SUN60IW2_PLL_VE0_REG,
+		.hw.init	= CLK_HW_INIT_PARENTS_HW("pll-ve0",
+				pll_ref_hws, &ccu_nm_ops,
+				CLK_SET_RATE_UNGATE),
+	},
+};
+
+static const struct clk_hw *pll_ve0_hws[] = { &pll_ve0_clk.common.hw };
+
+static struct ccu_nm pll_ve1_clk = {
+	.enable		= BIT(31),
+	.lock		= BIT(28),
+	.n		= _SUNXI_CCU_MULT_OFFSET_MIN_MAX(8, 8, 1, 12, 255),
+	.m		= _SUNXI_CCU_DIV(1, 1),
+	.min_rate	= SUN60IW2_PLL_VE_MIN_VCO,
+	.max_rate	= SUN60IW2_PLL_VE_MAX_VCO,
+	.common		= {
+		.reg		= SUN60IW2_PLL_VE1_REG,
+		.hw.init	= CLK_HW_INIT_PARENTS_HW("pll-ve1",
+				pll_ref_hws, &ccu_nm_ops,
+				CLK_SET_RATE_UNGATE),
+	},
+};
+
+static const struct clk_hw *pll_ve1_hws[] = { &pll_ve1_clk.common.hw };
+
+/*
+ * The output taps, PLL_OUTPUT_DIV [22:20] (P0 = field + 1) behind
+ * PLL_OUTPUT_GATE bit 27. Named after the UM's own mux labels, "VE0PLL" and
+ * "VE1PLL", as npupll and gpupll are -- "pll-ve0" is
+ * the VCO, and the two names would otherwise differ by word order alone in a
+ * clk_summary dump. NOT critical: this gate is the one the consumer owns.
+ * CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT, so one clk_set_rate() on a module clock configures
+ * divider, tap and VCO.
+ */
+static SUNXI_CCU_M_HWS_WITH_GATE(ve0pll_clk, "ve0pll", pll_ve0_hws,
+				 SUN60IW2_PLL_VE0_REG, 20, 3, BIT(27),
+				 CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT);
